    "finish_elapsed_s": 10.13659581396496,
    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it absorbs heat from inside the fridge and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas travels through coils behind or under the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen. Finally, the refrigerant cools down, turns back into a liquid, and repeats the cycle. Because heat keeps getting removed, the inside stays cold. The fridge doesn't \"make cold\"—it just moves heat out!"
